@@ -0,0 +1,218 @@
+# Automated GitHub Code Search and Repository Intelligence for TypeScript Agents
+
+## Executive Summary
+
+For agent-driven research across TypeScript repositories, the most important distinction is between **GitHub’s web code search experience** and **GitHub’s public APIs/CLI for code search**. GitHub’s web code search supports regular expressions, boolean operators, qualifiers such as `repo:`, `org:`, `language:`, `path:`, `content:`, and `symbol:`, and symbol search for TypeScript via Tree-sitter-backed parsing. By contrast, GitHub’s public API-backed code search remains tied to the legacy code-search engine: the GitHub CLI explicitly says the results may differ from `github.com`, and that newer features such as regex search are not yet available via the GitHub API. That gap is the single biggest reason many serious agent workflows adopt a secondary indexer instead of relying on GitHub’s official code-search API alone. citeturn39view0turn5view4turn11search10
+
+Official GitHub APIs are still valuable, but today they are better thought of as a **discovery-and-metadata layer** than as a full-fidelity agent retrieval layer. The REST Search API gives you searchable code results with repository/path/SHA/text-match metadata, but it only searches the default branch, only indexes files smaller than 384 KB, caps search results at 1,000, and the docs currently show a notable inconsistency on the code-search endpoint’s authenticated rate limit: one section says 9 requests per minute, while the endpoint section says 10 requests per minute. GitHub GraphQL adds flexible metadata retrieval and a generic `search` field with up to 1,000 results, but its value for code search is primarily around joining search results to richer repository intelligence under GraphQL’s points-based rate model rather than replacing a real regex-capable code index. citeturn6view0turn12search3turn12search0turn38view0
+
+If you want the strongest out-of-the-box platform for large multi-repo TypeScript search and agent context, **Sourcegraph** is the most complete hosted option in the materials reviewed: it has regex and symbol search, repo/file/language/revision filters, commit/diff search, code insights, MCP/API/CLI access, and managed plus self-hosted deployment; its public pricing page currently starts at **$16K** for the enterprise plan. For low-friction public-repo agent work, **searchcode.com** is unusually agent-native: it exposes MCP and REST APIs, supports regex/boolean/structural code search over public Git repositories, and is currently free during beta. For public-code-only ad hoc discovery, **grep.app** and **livegrep** are useful, but they are materially weaker for authenticated, private-repo, or enterprise automation than Sourcegraph or self-hosted engines. citeturn16search1turn15search0turn24search10turn25search0turn22view1turn22view0
+
+For self-hosted/privacy-sensitive deployments, the best choices depend on whether you want a **purpose-built code search engine** or a **build-your-own search platform**. **Zoekt** and **OpenGrok** are the strongest open-source code-search-specific engines in the reviewed material. Zoekt supports fast substring and regex search plus repo sync/indexing services and an API/webserver. OpenGrok is a mature Java-based source browser and cross-reference engine with container deployment and an OpenAPI specification in-repo. A custom **Elasticsearch** stack is the most flexible if you want hybrid lexical/semantic retrieval and large-scale control, but it comes with the highest implementation burden because you must design the code document schema, ingest/indexing pipeline, ranking, and repo/line metadata yourself. A **ripgrep-based local clone cache** is still the lowest-friction private option, but it is not a real central index and scales poorly once you need many repositories, history-aware metadata, or multi-agent concurrency. citeturn26view0turn27view0turn28view0turn31search14turn31search0turn29view0
+
+My bottom-line recommendations are straightforward. For **quick discovery with minimal setup**, use GitHub web code search plus GitHub REST/GraphQL for metadata enrichment, optionally adding searchcode.com for agent-friendly public-repo drilling. For **continuous indexing across many repos**, use Sourcegraph if you can accept a commercial platform. For **privacy-sensitive self-hosting**, choose Zoekt when you want fast code search with moderate implementation effort, or Elasticsearch only when you specifically need custom ranking, hybrid semantic retrieval, or deep integration with your existing search stack. Pair any of these with GitHub webhooks, GitHub App auth, and a small incremental index-update pipeline instead of polling. citeturn36view0turn32search0turn32search3turn32search16turn16search1turn26view0turn31search14
+
+## Official GitHub Capabilities
+
+GitHub’s **current web code search** is strong for manual and browser-based investigation. It supports exact phrases, boolean operators, regular expressions delimited by slashes, and qualifiers such as `repo:`, `org:`, `user:`, `enterprise:`, `language:`, `path:`, `content:`, and `is:`. It also supports `symbol:` search, and GitHub explicitly lists **TypeScript** among the symbol-extraction-supported languages. This is especially valuable for TypeScript repository intelligence because symbol search lets you jump from “find files containing a token” to “find actual definitions,” which sharply reduces noise for concepts such as `Dataset`, `RdfStore`, `KnowledgeGraph`, `EmbeddingStore`, or `ContractAnalyzer`. GitHub notes that symbol search is based on Tree-sitter and does not require build-tool integration. citeturn39view0
+
+The catch is that **GitHub’s official API surface does not yet match the web experience**. The REST Search API supports code search with the same broad family of qualifiers as the web interface, and it can return `text_matches` when you request the `application/vnd.github.text-match+json` media type. The endpoint returns up to 100 results per page, supports pagination, and is restricted to the default branch. It only searches files smaller than 384 KB, requires at least one search term, caps each search at 1,000 results overall, and the general Search API limits each query to 256 characters plus at most five boolean operators. GitHub also limits scope to at most 4,000 repositories per query and may set `incomplete_results: true` when a query times out. Those constraints matter for agent workflows because they strongly favor **narrow, repo-scoped, high-signal queries** instead of broad global mining. citeturn6view0
+
+The CLI reflects the same limitation. `gh search code` supports repo, owner, language, filename, extension, content-vs-path matching, JSON output, and `jq` filtering. The returned JSON fields include `path`, `repository`, `sha`, `textMatches`, and `url`, which is convenient for automation. But GitHub’s own CLI documentation says these results are powered by the **legacy code search engine**, that they may not match the results on `github.com`, and that newer features like regex search are not yet available via the API. For agent integration, that means `gh search code` is useful for shell-first automation and prototyping, but it should not be treated as a parity layer for modern GitHub web code search. citeturn5view3turn5view4
+
+GitHub GraphQL has a different role. The GraphQL API exposes a generic `search` field that uses GitHub search syntax and returns a maximum of 1,000 results. Its rate model is points-based rather than request-count-based; for example, user-authenticated requests generally get 5,000 points per hour, enterprise-owned GitHub App installations can get 10,000 points per hour, and a `GITHUB_TOKEN` in Actions gets 1,000 points per hour per repository for most repositories. GraphQL also has node-count limits and secondary limits on concurrency and per-minute points. In practice, GraphQL is best used to **join repository intelligence**—owners, topics, default branches, security settings, languages, dependency graph context, pull requests, discussions, audit data—onto a search pipeline, not as the primary engine for regex-heavy code retrieval. That is an inference from the combination of GraphQL search’s generic structure and GitHub’s explicit note that API-backed code search remains legacy-powered. citeturn12search3turn12search0turn38view0turn5view4
+
+For repository intelligence beyond string search, **GitHub Code Security and code scanning** are highly relevant. GitHub documents code scanning as a repository analysis feature that can use CodeQL or third-party tools, and its workflow configuration for CodeQL explicitly supports `javascript-typescript` for JavaScript and TypeScript repositories. Code scanning is available on public repositories and on organization-owned repositories with GitHub Code Security enabled on Team, Enterprise Cloud, or Enterprise Server. This makes GitHub code scanning a strong complement to code search: use search to locate candidate implementations and code scanning/CodeQL to identify higher-confidence security and correctness findings. GitHub also tracks code scanning upload rate limits separately, which is useful if your agents upload SARIF at scale. citeturn14search4turn14search1turn14search13turn35search8turn36view3
+
+On the enterprise side, GitHub offers both **GitHub Enterprise Cloud** and **GitHub Enterprise Server**. The public pricing page currently lists Enterprise as starting at **$21 per user/month**, and GitHub positions **GitHub Advanced Security / GitHub Code Security** as add-ons on Team and Enterprise. Enterprise Cloud adds features such as data residency, Enterprise Managed Users, SCIM provisioning, centralized enterprise account management, repository rules, and Audit Log API access. Enterprise Server is the self-hosted GitHub offering, and GitHub’s admin docs state that GHES uses **Elasticsearch to power search features**. GHES can also be connected to Enterprise Cloud via GitHub Connect for **unified search** across environments. For organizations that need GitHub-native governance and auditability, these enterprise capabilities are important—but they still do not remove the official API’s current code-search parity gap. citeturn40view0turn14search12turn35search2turn35search16turn35search1turn14search15
+
+Two operational footnotes are worth calling out. First, GitHub’s REST docs currently contain a **documentation inconsistency**: the “About search” section says authenticated code search is limited to 9 requests per minute, while the endpoint-specific “Search code” section says 10 requests per minute. Second, GitHub’s own REST best-practices guide recommends avoiding polling, using authenticated requests, avoiding concurrent requests, pausing between mutative requests, using conditional requests where appropriate, and not ignoring errors. For agent systems, the right response is not to hardcode assumptions, but to inspect rate-limit headers, `/rate_limit`, and build for graceful degradation. citeturn6view0turn36view0turn36view3
+
+## Tool Landscape and Comparison
+
+The current landscape falls into three buckets. **Official GitHub** is the natural system of record and is excellent for permissions, metadata, webhooks, and governance, but currently weak for regex-capable API search. **Hosted code-search platforms** differ sharply in maturity: Sourcegraph is the only reviewed option that is clearly enterprise-grade for large authenticated codebases and agent integration; searchcode.com is the most explicitly agent-native for public repositories; grep.app and livegrep are useful public code-search services but not full private-repo intelligence platforms in the reviewed materials. **Self-hosted engines** are the right answer when privacy, data residency, or cost control dominate, but their deployment and maintenance burden varies widely. citeturn16search1turn25search0turn22view1turn22view0turn26view0turn27view0turn31search14
+
+The most important hosted option is **Sourcegraph**. Its query language supports RE2 regex, repo/file/language filters, revision search, symbol selection, commit and diff search, and result-type selection such as `select:repo`, `select:file`, and `select:symbol`. Its product pages emphasize code search, symbol search, commit/diff search, search contexts, code insights, MCP server, GraphQL/REST APIs, and CLI access. Its current pricing page lists a single enterprise plan starting at **$16K**, with single-tenant cloud and self-hosted deployment options. For agent integration, that combination of regex search, revision-aware indexing, and official MCP/API access is unusually strong. citeturn15search0turn24search10turn16search1turn18search4turn17search7
+
+The most interesting newer agent-oriented service is **searchcode.com**. Its public materials position it as “code intelligence for LLMs,” with MCP and REST APIs, six structured tools, repository analysis, code search, file retrieval, findings retrieval, and public GitHub repository support. It explicitly advertises regex, boolean queries, and structural filters, and it is currently **free during beta** with enterprise deployment available for private code. For public-repo exploratory research, especially when you want structured outputs instead of raw HTML or raw file fetching, it is promising. The obvious limitation is that its public beta positioning means long-term pricing and enterprise maturity are not yet as settled as Sourcegraph’s. citeturn25search0
+
+**livegrep** and **grep.app** are valuable as public-code search surfaces, but their automation posture is much thinner in the reviewed materials. livegrep’s public UI supports `repo:`, `path:`, negative variants, `max_matches:`, and regex via RE2 syntax. grep.app positions itself as code, file, and path search across a million GitHub repositories. Both are useful for discovery. However, in the primary materials reviewed here, neither presents Sourcegraph-like private-code indexing, enterprise governance, or a documented authenticated code-search product for private GitHub estates. That makes them good **adjunct discovery tools**, not strong primary backends for serious autonomous agents over private repositories. citeturn22view0turn22view1turn23search1
+
+Several names the user mentioned are, in practice, more naturally classified as **self-hosted engines than hosted SaaS services**. Hound’s README centers on building binaries or running a Docker image, configuring repositories, and using its up-to-date per-repository indexes plus “minimal API.” OpenGrok’s official materials center on installation/setup and container deployment, and its repository contains an `openapi.yaml`, indicating an API surface, but the reviewed materials do not present a current managed SaaS offering. Zoekt similarly exposes command-based usage plus indexserver/webserver services, including code-host sync and an API/webserver for search. That means Hound/OpenGrok/Zoekt are compelling if you want control and can operate infrastructure, but they should not be mistaken for turnkey hosted services. citeturn21view1turn27view0turn28view0turn26view0
+
+A custom **Elasticsearch** stack belongs in a different category. Elasticsearch is a distributed search engine with REST APIs, Query DSL, and regex support via `regexp` and `query_string`, plus vector and hybrid search capabilities. That makes it the most flexible foundation for code search plus semantic retrieval, but unlike Zoekt or OpenGrok it does not inherently understand repositories, files, symbols, or TypeScript syntax. You have to define the code document model yourself—for example, repository, revision, path, language, symbol table, line offsets, chunk embeddings, owners, and commit metadata. That gives you tremendous latitude, but also substantially higher engineering cost and more ways to get ranking wrong. citeturn31search14turn30view3turn31search0turn31search2
