+
+## TypeScript Search Patterns
+
+Good TypeScript repository research starts by distinguishing **repository discovery**, **dependency discovery**, **implementation discovery**, and **symbol discovery**. For the domains you care about—Semantic Web, ontologies, NLP, knowledge graphs, agent memory, and Legal AI—the highest-signal patterns are usually a mix of package imports, W3C namespace IRIs, query-language terms, data-model symbol names, and path/file conventions such as `src/`, `packages/*`, `ontology/`, `sparql/`, `graph/`, `memory/`, or `legal/`. GitHub web code search supports the needed qualifier and regex syntax for this style of work; Sourcegraph can express the same ideas with richer repo/revision controls; GitHub’s official API cannot yet match the web regex experience. citeturn39view0turn15search0turn5view4
+
+A practical starting pattern set for **Semantic Web and RDF/OWL/PROV-O** in TypeScript is shown below. These examples intentionally combine lexical and structural signals so that an agent can quickly separate “mentions in docs” from “actual code.” citeturn39view0turn15search0
+
+| Research target | High-signal search pattern |
+|---|---|
+| RDFJS ecosystem usage | `language:TypeScript /from\\s+['"](?:@rdfjs\\/types|n3|rdf-ext|rdf-parse|sparqljs)['"]/` |
+| W3C RDF/RDFS/OWL namespace handling | `language:TypeScript content:"http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"` |
+| OWL or RDFS class/property modeling | `language:TypeScript /(subClassOf|ObjectProperty|DatatypeProperty|AnnotationProperty|equivalentClass)/` |
+| PROV-O support | `language:TypeScript /(prov:|PROV-O|wasDerivedFrom|wasGeneratedBy|used\\b)/` |
+| SPARQL implementation code | `language:TypeScript /(SELECT|CONSTRUCT|ASK|DESCRIBE)\\s+.*WHERE/ path:/src/**/*.ts` |
+| Quad/dataset factories | `language:TypeScript /(namedNode|blankNode|literal|quad|dataset)\\(/` |
+| ontology manifests | `path:/package\\.json$/ (rdf OR owl OR sparql OR ontology OR shacl)` |
+
+For **ontologies and knowledge graphs**, the best queries tend to pivot between graph-query vocabulary and schema vocabulary. In TypeScript repositories, imports of graph libraries or SPARQL/Cypher/Gremlin tooling are often more precise than generic words like “graph.” These patterns are good first passes: `language:TypeScript /from\\s+['"](?:graphology|neo4j|gremlin|sparqljs|n3)['"]/`, `language:TypeScript /(knowledge graph|entity linking|entity resolution|triple store|property graph)/`, `language:TypeScript /(cypher|gremlin|sparql)\\b/`, and `symbol:/^(?:Graph|KnowledgeGraph|TripleStore|EntityLinker|Ontology).*$/ language:TypeScript`. For GitHub web search specifically, `symbol:` is especially helpful for finding actual class/function definitions instead of references. citeturn39view0
+
+For **NLP** in TypeScript, imports and pipeline terminology are the highest-yield filters. Useful patterns include `language:TypeScript /from\\s+['"](?:compromise|wink-nlp|natural|node-nlp|langchain)['"]/`, `language:TypeScript /(tokeniz|lemmatiz|stemming|embedding|entity recogn|named entity|pos tag|vector store)/`, and `path:/src/**/*.ts /(PromptTemplate|Embeddings|Retriever|Rerank|Chunk|Splitter)/`. In practice, an agent should start with import-based queries, then widen to conceptual vocabulary only after it has repo candidates. That reduces false positives from README files and issue templates. This is an application of the qualifier and boolean/regex behavior documented by GitHub and Sourcegraph. citeturn39view0turn15search0
+
+For **agent memory**, the key is to search for both explicit memory terminology and the modern TypeScript agent-framework classes that embody it. Strong patterns are `language:TypeScript /(episodic memory|semantic memory|working memory|conversation memory|vector memory)/`, `language:TypeScript /(MemorySaver|BaseChatMessageHistory|VectorStoreRetrieverMemory|StateGraph|checkpoint)/`, and `language:TypeScript /from\\s+['"](?:langchain|@langchain\\/core|@langchain\\/community|chromadb|lancedb|pgvector)['"]/`. When you are mining many repos, add a path fence such as `path:/src/**/*.ts OR path:/packages/**/*.ts` to drop docs/tests. citeturn39view0
+
+For **Legal AI**, the challenge is that many repositories use generic LLM infrastructure rather than explicitly legal package names. The best first-pass filters therefore mix domain language and implementation signposts: `language:TypeScript /(contract|clause|provision|statute|case law|citation|brief|holding|privilege|discovery)/ path:/src/**/*.ts`, `language:TypeScript /(citation extractor|bluebook|legal reasoning|document review|matter intake)/`, and `path:/package\\.json$/ (openai OR anthropic OR langchain) AND (legal OR contract OR clause OR policy)`. In this domain, agents should also inspect repository topics, README terms, and `package.json` descriptions via GraphQL/REST metadata enrichment rather than relying on code search alone. citeturn39view0turn38view0
+
+One pattern I strongly recommend for all six domains is the **two-pass search**. Pass one uses high-precision imports, namespace IRIs, or symbol names to find candidate repos. Pass two uses broader conceptual regexes inside those repos only. This strategy aligns well with GitHub REST’s repository-scope limitations, GitHub web qualifiers, and Sourcegraph/Zoekt’s fast repo-scoped filtering. In agent pipelines, it substantially lowers token spend and rate-limit pressure because you stop dumping irrelevant files into the model context. citeturn6view0turn39view0turn26view0
+
+## Agent Integration Architectures
+
+For autonomous agents, the central design decision is whether the search backend is **event-driven** or **poll-driven**. GitHub’s own REST best-practices guide says to avoid polling where possible and subscribe to webhook events instead. GitHub’s webhook best-practices page adds important operational constraints: subscribe only to events you need, use a webhook secret, use HTTPS, respond within 10 seconds, check event type and action, redeliver missed deliveries, and use the `X-GitHub-Delivery` header as an idempotency key. For code-search/indexing systems, that translates into a standard pattern: webhooks enqueue work immediately, acknowledge quickly, and let asynchronous workers fetch deltas and update indexes. citeturn36view0turn36view1turn32search11
+
+Authentication choice is equally important. For durable organization-scale automation, **GitHub Apps** are usually preferable to PATs. GitHub Apps can authenticate as the app with a JWT, then mint installation access tokens that expire after **one hour**; GitHub’s docs also note that Octokit can manage token generation and refresh for you. GitHub’s app best-practices recommend choosing the right token type: installation tokens for app-attributed automation, user tokens for user-attributed actions. Fine-grained PATs are still useful for personal or narrow-scoped tooling, but GitHub documents them as permission-bound and organization policy-controlled, and enterprise/org admins can restrict or review them. For agent systems that act independently and need repo-webhook integration, GitHub Apps are the safer default. citeturn32search12turn32search10turn32search4turn32search1turn32search0turn34search13turn33search2turn33search0
+
+Caching and incremental updates should be designed around **repository revision identity**, not around raw search responses alone. A clean pattern is: store repo default branch, last indexed commit SHA, last successful webhook delivery ID, and index version; when a push arrives, compute the new head, fetch only changed trees/blobs, and reindex incrementally. For GitHub REST/GraphQL calls that are still part of your metadata plane, use conditional requests where possible and inspect rate-limit headers. For GraphQL, monitor `cost`, remaining points, and node counts; for REST, watch both `search` and `code_search` buckets at `/rate_limit`. For search backends such as Sourcegraph, Zoekt, or Hound, prefer their own repository-sync/index services rather than rebuilding full indexes on every event. citeturn36view0turn36view3turn38view0turn26view0turn21view1
+
+Error handling needs to be explicit because agent systems tend to amplify transient failures. GitHub’s REST troubleshooting and rate-limit docs recommend respecting `retry-after` and reset windows. For GraphQL, exceeding primary limits can still return HTTP 200 with an error payload, while secondary limits may surface as 200 or 403. GitHub also enforces secondary limits on concurrency, per-minute points, and CPU time, so indiscriminate fan-out is a common integration mistake. The safest pattern is a small per-installation work queue, exponential backoff, dead-letter handling, and idempotent replays keyed by repo + revision + delivery ID. citeturn38view0turn36view2turn36view0
+
+Cost and rate-limit mitigation come from four tactics: **narrow queries**, **cache aggressively**, **separate metadata retrieval from content retrieval**, and **pre-index the repos you care about**. Searchcode.com’s pitch is essentially an extreme version of this idea for public repos: return structured repository intelligence rather than making the model repeatedly fetch/parse raw files. Sourcegraph’s value proposition is similar at enterprise scale. Even if you build your own stack, the same principle applies: agents should ask the search backend for ranked candidate files, symbols, and diff-localized context before they ask for full-file content. citeturn25search0turn16search1turn24search10
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+    A[GitHub App] --> B[Webhook Receiver]
+    B --> C[Queue]
+    C --> D[Metadata Worker]
+    C --> E[Index Worker]
+    D --> F[(GitHub REST + GraphQL cache)]
+    E --> G[(Sourcegraph or Searchcode or GitHub search)]
+    F --> H[Agent Orchestrator]
+    G --> H
+    H --> I[Ranked file/symbol candidates]
+    I --> J[LLM or coding agent]
+    J --> K[Report, patch, or PR suggestion]
+```
+
+The diagram above is the best pattern for **quick discovery and scalable hosted retrieval**: GitHub remains the source of truth for permissions and events, while the search backend provides fast candidate generation and the agent only pulls full content for shortlisted files. That separation is the highest-leverage architecture choice in this whole report. citeturn36view0turn32search3turn16search1turn25search0
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+    A[GitHub Enterprise Cloud or Server] --> B[Webhook Receiver]
+    B --> C[Queue]
+    C --> D[Mirror/Fetcher]
+    D --> E[(Bare repo cache)]
+    E --> F[(Zoekt or OpenGrok)]
+    E --> G[(Elasticsearch custom index)]
+    F --> H[Search API]
+    G --> H
+    H --> I[Policy and auth layer]
+    I --> J[Agent runtime]
+    J --> K[Human review or CI gate]
+```
+
+This second pattern is the right fit for **privacy-sensitive or self-hosted environments**. Zoekt or OpenGrok can be the primary lexical code-search layer; Elasticsearch can sit beside them when you need custom ranking, vector retrieval, or semantic overlays. The downside is operational: you now own fetch orchestration, index refresh, auth propagation, and observability. citeturn26view0turn27view0turn31search14
+
+## Recommendations and Workflows
+
+If your constraint is **quick discovery with low setup**, the best stack is: **GitHub web code search for interactive regex/symbol searching, GitHub REST/GraphQL for metadata enrichment, and searchcode.com for agent-friendly public-repo drilling**. This gives you excellent TypeScript repo discovery across your Semantic Web / ontology / KG / NLP / agent-memory / Legal AI target spaces with almost no infrastructure. The tradeoff is that private-repo automation remains limited unless you add GitHub-native permissions plus some other private-code index. citeturn39view0turn25search0turn6view0turn38view0
+
+If your constraint is **scalable continuous indexing for many repositories**, my recommendation is **Sourcegraph + GitHub App + webhook queue**. Sourcegraph is the most complete reviewed platform for regex, symbol, commit/diff search, code insights, MCP/API access, and deployment flexibility. Use GitHub for auth/events, Sourcegraph for retrieval, and keep a small metadata cache keyed by repo and revision. Add GitHub code scanning/CodeQL when you need security or correctness evidence in JavaScript/TypeScript repositories. This is the highest-confidence general recommendation in the report. citeturn16search1turn15search0turn24search10turn14search13turn14search4
+
+If your constraint is **privacy-sensitive self-hosting**, the best decision tree is: choose **Zoekt** if you want a search-focused engine with repo sync and API/webserver support; choose **OpenGrok** if you want a mature source browser/cross-reference experience and can live with a heavier Java deployment; choose **Elasticsearch** only if you explicitly need custom ranking, hybrid semantic retrieval, or integration with an existing Elastic estate. For small teams or one-off research, a **ripgrep clone cache** may be all you need initially, but it is not the right long-term multi-agent architecture. citeturn26view0turn27view0turn31search14turn29view0
+
+The most valuable **next steps**, in priority order, are these. First, decide whether your real bottleneck is **search quality**, **private-repo access**, or **operating complexity**; that choice determines whether you should stay GitHub-native, buy a platform, or self-host. Second, stand up a **GitHub App** and webhook receiver before you do anything else, because event-driven indexing solves rate limits and freshness better than any polling design. Third, test your actual domain queries against a representative TypeScript repo set and measure false-positive rates, especially for Legal AI and agent-memory concepts. Fourth, add a metadata cache and a reranking stage before sending file content into an LLM. Fifth, if you expect more than a few dozen repos or frequent agent runs, adopt an indexer with incremental updates rather than shelling out to GitHub search on demand. citeturn32search0turn32search3turn36view0turn39view0turn26view0
+
+A strong **example workflow for candidate-repo discovery** looks like this: search GitHub web code search or searchcode.com for import-based TypeScript patterns; use GitHub GraphQL or REST to fetch repo metadata, topics, default branch, and visibility; store the candidates; then run repo-scoped deeper searches for symbols, namespaces, and implementation terms. This keeps the first pass cheap and the second pass precise. citeturn39view0turn25search0turn38view0turn6view0
+
+A strong **example workflow for continuous intelligence** looks like this: receive `push` and pull-request webhooks from a GitHub App; queue a repo update; fetch changed refs and metadata; update Sourcegraph or Zoekt indexes; optionally run CodeQL/code scanning or upload SARIF from a companion analyzer; then let the agent query the index for implementation candidates, impacted symbols, and related diffs before it proposes a patch or research summary. This integrates search, security, and change intelligence without making the LLM your primary retrieval engine. citeturn32search3turn36view1turn14search4turn14search13turn26view0turn16search1
+
+Below are sample commands I would treat as the most useful starting points.
+
+**GitHub REST code search** is still useful for repo-scoped discovery and metadata extraction, even though it lacks regex parity with the modern web UI. citeturn6view0turn5view4
+
+```bash
+curl -L \
+  -H "Accept: application/vnd.github+json" \
+  -H "Authorization: Bearer $GITHUB_TOKEN" \
+  -H "X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2026-03-10" \
+  "https://api.github.com/search/code?q=from%20language:typescript%20repo:owner/repo%20path:src"
+```
+
+**GitHub CLI** is convenient when you want JSON fields that can be piped into `jq`. GitHub’s own docs say this command uses the legacy code-search engine, so I would not use it as my only retrieval layer. citeturn5view3turn5view4
+
+```bash
+gh search code "namedNode quad" \
+  --repo owner/repo \
+  --language TypeScript \
+  --json path,repository,sha,textMatches,url \
+  --jq '.[] | {repo: .repository.nameWithOwner, path, sha}'
+```
+
+**GitHub GraphQL** is ideal for metadata enrichment and rate-limit-aware repository intelligence. citeturn38view0turn12search3
+
+```bash
+curl -L \
+  -H "Authorization: Bearer $GITHUB_TOKEN" \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  https://api.github.com/graphql \
+  -d @- <<'JSON'
+{
+  "query": "query($q:String!) { search(query:$q, type: REPOSITORY, first: 10) { repositoryCount edges { node { ... on Repository { nameWithOwner isPrivate defaultBranchRef { name } stargazerCount } } } } rateLimit { cost remaining resetAt } }",
+  "variables": {
+    "q": "semantic web language:typescript"
+  }
+}
+JSON
+```
+
+**searchcode.com** is a compelling public-repo REST option for structured agent retrieval. citeturn25search0
+
+```bash
+curl -X POST \
+  "https://api.searchcode.com/api/v1/code_search?client=my-agent" \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{
+    "repository":"https://github.com/rdfjs/types",
+    "query":"namedNode OR quad",
+    "language":"TypeScript"
+  }'
+```
+
+**Zoekt** is the cleanest command-line starting point for self-hosted open-source search. citeturn26view0
+
+```bash
+go install github.com/sourcegraph/zoekt/cmd/zoekt-git-index@latest
+go install github.com/sourcegraph/zoekt/cmd/zoekt@latest
+
+zoekt-git-index -index ~/.zoekt /path/to/local/repo
+zoekt 'namedNode file:\.ts$'
+```
+
+**ripgrep** remains the fastest low-friction fallback for local clone caches and scripted repo sweeps. citeturn29view0
+
+```bash
+rg -n --glob '**/*.ts' '(namedNode|blankNode|quad|dataset)\(' /repos
+rg -n --glob '**/*.ts' '(contract|clause|citation|statute|brief)' /repos
+rg -n --glob '**/*.ts' '(MemorySaver|BaseChatMessageHistory|VectorStoreRetrieverMemory)' /repos
+```
+
+Taken together, the evidence points to one practical conclusion: if your goal is a serious agent system over TypeScript GitHub repositories in the domains you listed, the winning pattern is **GitHub for auth/events/metadata, plus a secondary search index for retrieval quality**. The only times I would recommend staying entirely GitHub-native are when the repo set is small, regex parity is not important, or procurement and security constraints make every extra component harder than tolerating weaker search. citeturn5view4turn6view0turn39view0turn16search1turn26view0
